{
  "sn55.42:0.1": "Linked Discourses 55.42 ",
  "sn55.42:0.2": "5. Overflowing Merit, With Verses ",
  "sn55.42:0.3": "Overflowing Merit (2nd) ",
  "sn55.42:1.1": "“Mendicants, there are these four kinds of overflowing merit, overflowing goodness that nurture happiness. ",
  "sn55.42:1.2": "What four? ",
  "sn55.42:1.3": "It’s when a noble disciple has experiential confidence in the Buddha … ",
  "sn55.42:1.4": "",
  "sn55.42:1.5": "",
  "sn55.42:2.1": "the teaching … ",
  "sn55.42:2.2": "the Saṅgha … ",
  "sn55.42:3.1": "Furthermore, a noble disciple lives at home with heart rid of the stain of stinginess, freely generous, open-handed, loving to let go, committed to charity, loving to give and to share. ",
  "sn55.42:3.2": "This is the fourth kind of overflowing merit, overflowing goodness that nurtures happiness. ",
  "sn55.42:3.3": "These are the four kinds of overflowing merit, overflowing goodness that nurture happiness. ",
  "sn55.42:4.1": "When a noble disciple has these four kinds of overflowing merit and goodness, it’s not easy to count how much merit they have by saying that ",
  "sn55.42:4.2": "this is the extent of their overflowing merit, overflowing goodness that nurtures happiness. ",
  "sn55.42:4.3": "It’s simply reckoned as an incalculable, immeasurable, great mass of merit. ",
  "sn55.42:5.1": "There are places where the great rivers—the Ganges, Yamunā, Aciravatī, Sarabhū, and Mahī—join together and converge. ",
  "sn55.42:5.2": "It’s not easy to count how much water